The pinout is also the one used by Intel on their version of a Pentium
motherboard. However, if you are not faint at heart and are handy with
soldering tools, you can do what I did. Remove the AT keyboard connector
from the motherboard, (A jewler's saw works very nicely here, less chance
of overheating the board when desoldering the connector.) clean out the
plated through holes for both the keyboard connector and the PS/2 mouse
connector next to it. Insert PS/2 style connectors in both positions and
solder them back in. Due care is called for as you can easily destroy the
board if you are not carefull. Once you complete this you have the ps/2
version of the board, which incidently when loaded with SRM firmware is now
capable of running Digital UNIX. Don't try to use a Compaq mouse, I have
not been able to get them to work (ironic eh?). I have found that DEC
rodents, naturally work, and a dirt cheap $8.00 three button mouse called
Today's Mouse works just fine and is quite comfortable. I have also found
that the Belkin keyboard/mouse/monitor switch (one alpha is never enough),
works very well with the axppci.

Tom Langley
Richmond, VA
